Sure, a lot of problems can be solved with the right policies and enough money. But in the case of UK higher education, I doubt they can easily reverse things like Brexit, the over-reliance on international students, policies that make the UK less attractive to those same students, the persistent underfunding of teaching and research, and the UK’s overall economic stagnation. With the NHS, public services, and infrastructure already under severe strain, I don’t see the government suddenly allocating serious money to higher education in any sustained way. I also don’t think the full effects are visible yet; they will probably only become clear over the next decade or two.
Quoting @YuDai_Tsai
@miniapeur @AndyBurnhamGM I agree to a degree, but the UK’s higher education problems could be solved by a single policy — or even by one wealthy individual. The real question is whether there is enough motivation to do so.

